@punxsutawneyphilofficial As long as you are free to leave your job then there's no reason to hate your boss for making way more money. If you feel exploited by your boss, then you should try make your own business or find other jobs that you are comfortable in. Risky? Yes, that's what your boss went through before having a successful business.

Just for context, I have been working for the same company for the past 6 years without any increase in salary despite the company have been growing exponentially. However, instead of blaming my boss, I started looking for another job which made me realized I couldn't get better pay elsewhere. I didn't stop trying though, I started my own side gig selling some stuff online which I do need to spent some of my savings. I still couldn't leave my job though as my own side hustle is not yet enough to cover my expenses. That'd be my dream to not having to work for someone else someday.

What I want to say here is stop having victim mentality, its your own life. You could have a better life but don't expect other people to give that to you, You need to work hard(and smart) for it.

@DrDre9998282 Sure, it is good to try to take control of your life, but it doesn't mean things shouldn't be more fair or easier to actually do it. Also, the amount of risk a person takes in starting a business depends a lot on their background. While of course there are some that come up from nothing and take huge risks, a lot of business owners also come from some amount of money. Some people can get a loan from parents or get good business connections or have good enough credit to get their own loan. Even putting that aside, there's a difference between failing meaning that tail between your legs, you have to go live with your parents a while until you get back on your feet. Versus someone failing means they are literally homeless and might die out on the streets. Plenty of people work just as hard or harder just to keep their head above water, than people who happened to have better luck.

I say this as someone who happened to be in the right place at the right time, being good at computers and programming and get along well with most people, and so make a lot of money now. I still have some disadvantages compared to others for my personal situation in terms of supporting my mom, so I'm paying two mortgages and two sets of utilities, but I still have disposable income and I don't need to work as hard as someone in retail would be. It's great that I was able to come from growing up on welfare to having a very good income, but that was a combo of gov support helping early on and then a lot of luck after that. I certainly didn't use some crazy drive and impeccable planning get myself to where I am now (if I had that, I'd probably be making even more instead).

Anyway, I get annoyed by this idea that all business owners are some incredible people who somehow worked harder and made more sacrifices than regular employees. Often it just isn't the case.

