@JollyRancher22 I get what you’re saying, but my point is that a lot of fast fashion is made in the same factories, by the same workers, as higher priced brands, so price alone doesn’t automatically mean better quality or ethics. I also understand your point about not shopping based on brand names or price, but instead looking for companies that try to source things ethically. 

What people often miss, though, is that the average person can’t afford to drop $150 on a single sweater. Expecting people to do that isn’t realistic, and it can come across as classist (not saying you are classist, but that's how it can sound). It’s great if that works for your budget, but that’s almost my entire grocery budget for the week.

I thrift most of my clothes, but some of them are from fast fashion brands because that’s what I can afford. I take care of my things, and I’ve had plenty of so called “poor quality” fast fashion items last me for years. I’d much rather buy a $10 sweater from a thrift store that will last me just as long, because I actually value and take care of my clothing.

