@nikolpavlova268 Because it's not actually expensive, but rather the fair price for all the effort that goes into producing a piece of garment, all the way from farming to sewing and the many steps that occur in-between (harvesting, fibre conditioning, dying, designing, cutting, washing, ironing...). If you had to do all these steps by yourself, I bet you'd think getting paid less than $3/day was slavery. And it is. But because it is out of sight, people don't care they're actually supporting it.

Also, if you need to buy a new T-shirt every few weeks, maybe you should reevaluate what need is it actually filling. Because I've worn the same clothes for a decade at a time and no one has accused me of "smelling" (as if washing machines didn't exist) or questioned my fashion choices. Truth is, if you have a personality of your own, people don't care you used the same dress twice. Even the wife of the most powerful president at the time (Michelle Obama) did.

Learn how to care for your clothes, how to sew a button and how to mend a hole. Each takes less than five minutes, but your clothes will last years upon years of service.

