@fallacyfallout6985 Yes, the worse part is it is physiological\neurological. Based on book "The Tell-Tale Brain" by V. S. Ramachandran (one who came up with mirror therapy for phantom limbs), pain in others results in "null  response" . That means, when you see a person getting pricked, your nervous system does exactly what that person's nervous system is doing, including sending all the messages. But the last check, "is this really me?",  fails. Which is why you don't *feel* pain.
That means, no matter what you do, you can *never* feel what that child feels working in that sweat shop 12 hours a day unless you have experienced that kind of pain (12 hour shifts at age 15 for years without breaks or holidays). You can have sympathy. You can even have empathy. Not actual feeling.
So, best we can do is ask ourselves, why am I doing this?

