@Peridot-banana In a very real sense, much of this impulse to seek purity seems to stem from a sort of internalized trauma people inherit from (quite specifically) socially conservative upbringing. The idea of self-flagellating asceticism as a form of purification seems to be hand in hand with hierarchical conservative ideology - fear of the unknown manifesting as a desire to render things controlled and predictable, often by punishing that which falls out of line. By imposing Order upon oneself through self-denial, one may reclaim a semblance of control over the chaotic and confusing outside world.

Even people who consider themselves progressive often internalize such mindsets, and more or less manifest the core behaviors even as they're reframed in the aesthetics of radical change.

At the end of the day systemic problems require systemic solutions, not the cultivation of a personal sense of virtue.

