@SoulDevoured where I live a cheap pair of new shoes is easily $80-$100, especially since the shoes we were specifically referring to in that conversation were boots, which are more expensive overall. Maybe it's different in other countries/cities but not here; I don't think I've ever seen a pair of brand new shoes for under $50 unless they were sandals or like these really cheap sneakers that fall apart after three wears that aren't very practical/have very thin soles and normally have fun designs so you can't wear them in day to day work life anyway. The closest Walmart is like 2ish hours away on the bus, and the one time I went there was because I was desperate for something specific and I still wound up paying $50+. If you want shoes for $30 you go to a thrift store. I'm speaking from that perspective, and the perspective of a person who makes barely above minimum wage, who has been desperate, who has bought fast fashion in a pinch, but chooses not to when I'm just looking for clothes and as a result I spend less on clothes than most of the people I know because I focus on thrift stores and good quality when I'm shopping new. I'm willing to pay more upfront because I've found as a result I pay less in the long run. 

If somebody is so desperate that an extra $30 will break the bank, they can do what they want. But I hear complaints all the time from friends of mine who have worked steadily at the same gig for years, where they've gotten raises and benefits, about their clothes falling apart while I have hiking shoes from 2018 that I paid $180 for that lasted until just a few months ago and I wore those almost every day for months on end at some points, and I paid 60/year. 

Compare that to people who pay for the cheapest ones every year and they spend $200-300 in one year because they go to the store 3 times a year to get JUST the same kinds of shoes. That's what I'm talking about. That's the situation where I live, my own experience. Again, they can do what they want if they chose to. I just don't see the point when a better option is available to them that costs more upfront but less than in the long run. Yes, it's a big ask, and yes, I cringed when I paid another $180 on hiking shoes a few months ago, but I've worn these almost everyday since and there has been no issue so far, so I consider it worthwhile. I'm an immensely priviledged person in a lot of ways, but I'm not a wealthy person. That was my point. I don't think they are seeing where I'm coming from.

